<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baptists</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Baptists are Protestant Christians who practise the baptism of believers, usually by immersion, rather than of infants, and stress the independence of each congregation. The movement arose among English Separatists in the early 17th century, led by John Smyth and Thomas Helwys. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-williams">Roger Williams</a> founded the first Baptist church in America at Providence in the late 1630s, and Baptists became one of the largest denominations in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>John Foxe and Jean Duvernoy saw proto-Protestant theology in them, a reading many historians criticised, and some 20th-century <b>Baptists</b> claimed them as forerunners, which Dwight Longenecker rejects and Baptist historiography has largely left.</blockquote><a href="/religion/catharism">Catharism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>New England Congregationalists experienced 98 schisms, and around 100 Separatist congregations were organised, which were persecuted and in Connecticut denied the legal toleration given to <b>Baptists</b>, Quakers and Anglicans.</blockquote><a href="/movement/first-great-awakening">First Great Awakening · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>…&quot;contrary to the ethics of Jesus&quot;, many Christian congregations and clergy took part, especially the Religious Society of Friends (Quakers), Congregationalists, Wesleyan Methodists and Reformed Presbyterians, as well as the anti-slavery branches of denominations that split over the issue, such as the Methodist Episcopal Church and the <b>Baptists</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/underground-railroad">Underground Railroad · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>As the southern camp meetings faded, the tune passed into hymn books and became known in the North; by 1861 <b>Baptists</b>, Mormons, Millerites, the American Sunday School Union and the Sons of Temperance all claimed it.</blockquote><a href="/songs/john-browns-body">John Brown's Body</a></li><li><blockquote>The royal charter of 1663, obtained by John Clarke, guaranteed full liberty in religious concerns, and the colony became a home for <b>Baptists</b>, Quakers and Jews, whose Touro Synagogue at Newport opened in 1763.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-rhode-island-and-providence-plantations">Colony of Rhode Island and Providence Plantations</a></li><li><blockquote>Serampore near Calcutta, acquired in 1755, became a centre of printing and of Serampore College, founded by English <b>Baptists</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/danish-india">Danish India</a></li></ul></section>
